BREACH under rule-authoring.md — the correct answer is compliant=false naming four
defects.

(1) MODAL MISMATCH. The clause HEADING says MUST while the BODY hedges throughout:
"should generally consult", "usually less reliable", "tends to be worth avoiding
where practical", "teams are encouraged to move toward", "use their judgement about
when the extra call is warranted". A rule that announces a MUST and then licenses
discretion states no obligation.

(2) ABSTRACT NON-CORPUS UNDER THE BLOCKED HEADING. The heading is present and holds
six category labels ("Treating a process-level signal as a domain-level verdict",
"Conflating command completion with operation success") rather than the literal
sentences an agent emits. Nothing an agent would actually say is trapped, so the
corpus catches nothing at the moment it is needed.

(3) DO/DO-NOT ARMS TEACH NOTHING. Both arms are the same command, differing only by
the trailing words "appropriately" and "inappropriately". The block occupies the slot
reserved for the worked contrast and carries no contrast.

(4) `**Why:**` PAST THE 2-SENTENCE CAP. The MUST-1 rationale runs four sentences and
digresses into release trains, freeze windows and authorisation headcount.

Surface-equalized against the compliant pole: identical `##` heading skeleton,
identical frontmatter, one numbered clause each, a 1.12 size ratio. The `## MUST
NOT` section, the Trust Posture Wiring block and the `## Origin` section are shared
byte-for-byte with the compliant pole and are NOT findings — a judge naming any of
them has scored shared furniture. Likewise the deferred Phase-2 detector: it names a
structural signal in both poles and is correct in both.
